WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.088
The third change is delegation.

2
00:00:02.088 --> 00:00:06.562
In Active Directory, who can manage
what was largely expressed by OUs.

3
00:00:06.562 --> 00:00:11.900
Entra ID has no OUs, so delegation
has to be rebuilt on purpose.

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.112
The program uses three tiers.

5
00:00:14.112 --> 00:00:19.138
Tier zero is tenant-wide control:
Global Administrator and similar roles,

6
00:00:19.138 --> 00:00:22.763
plus break-glass accounts,
all activated just in time.

7
00:00:22.763 --> 00:00:27.725
Tier one is service administration,
such as Exchange, Intune and security.

8
00:00:27.725 --> 00:00:30.412
Tier two is departmental administration,

9
00:00:30.412 --> 00:00:33.637
like the help desk, scoped
to administrative units.

10
00:00:33.637 --> 00:00:38.313
The platform sets real limits, and the
old design documents got several wrong.

11
00:00:38.313 --> 00:00:40.725
Administrative units can't be nested.

12
00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:43.738
Groups that hold roles can't
have dynamic membership.

13
00:00:43.738 --> 00:00:47.099
Reader roles like Global Reader
apply to the whole tenant.

14
00:00:47.099 --> 00:00:52.462
And restricted management is chosen when a
unit is created; it can't be added later.

15
00:00:52.462 --> 00:00:56.708
It also takes those users and
groups out of Entra ID Governance:

16
00:00:56.708 --> 00:01:00.137
no access reviews or
lifecycle workflows for them.

17
00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:05.125
So the program's decision is that administrative
units are unrestricted by default.

18
00:01:05.125 --> 00:01:09.237
Restricted management is a per-unit
choice for sensitive accounts,

19
00:01:09.237 --> 00:01:12.240
recorded in the registry
before the unit exists.
